+ Tool Use

++ Tool-Use Loop
+++ Six-step cycle
++++_ 1 Define schema — name, description, input_schema
++++_ 2 Send message — tool definitions plus user input
++++_ 3 **tool_use** — name, unique ID, args; stop_reason: tool_use
++++_ 4 Execute tool — app runs it; assistant turn already ended
++++_ 5 Return result — **tool_result** carrying the same ID
++++_ 6 Claude continues — another tool call or a final turn
+++_ Ownership: Claude selects, app executes; model stateless between calls
+++_ Failure: skipped step 4 breaks the loop; systematic miss means fix schema <<failure>>

++ Message Blocks
+++ Four block types
++++_ text — Claude prose; preserve the full content array
++++_ tool_use — assistant call: tool name, unique ID, input args
++++_ tool_result — user turn: matching ID, content, optional is_error
++++_ thinking — extended thinking only; pass back unchanged
+++ Pairing invariant
++++_ Every tool_use needs a tool_result in the immediately following user turn
++++_ IDs must match; missing or late results fail API validation
++++_ Structural rule — prompt edits cannot repair it <<failure>>

++ Schema Anatomy
+++_ Name — short and specific (get_account_balance, not get_data)
+++ Description
++++_ State when to use and when not to use
++++_ Vague "find information" yields wrong selections <<failure>>
++++_ Exclusion conditions give Claude a decision rule
+++ input_schema
++++_ JSON Schema parameters the function accepts
++++_ required: inputs the call cannot work without
++++_ optional: defaults; absence can carry meaning

++ Schema Design Choices
+++_ Subtask dependency — sequence dependent calls; parallel independent ones
+++_ Required fields — required array; avoid fabricated values
+++_ Optional fields — defaults in signature; avoid forced guessing
+++_ Description length — 3–4 sentences: what, when, returns; add input examples
+++_ Overlapping parameter types — name the domain or trigger in each description

-- Disambiguation Example
--- Problem: two similar tools
----_ search_knowledge_base vs get_cached_result
----_ Both described as "use this to find information" <<failure>>
----_ Distinct names alone did not prevent wrong picks
--- Fix: exclusion conditions
----_ search_knowledge_base — skip if a prior search covers the question
----_ get_cached_result — only after a same-query search this session
---_ Requires complete conversation history in every request
--- Fit check
----_ Handles well: specific triggers plus explicit exclusions
----_ Poor fit: near-identical tools — merge with a type parameter <<failure>>

-- MCP Alternative
--- What it is: server-owned definitions and execution
--- Loop unchanged: ListToolsRequest yields tools; same tool_use/result rules
--- Context cost: unused definitions still consume window budget
--- Connector controls (mcp_toolset)
----_ defer_loading — defer definition loading until needed
----_ enabled — allow or deny specific tools
----_ Requires mcp-client-2025-11-20 beta header <<failure>>
--- Transports
----_ Local stdio subprocess; API connector cannot reach it <<failure>>
----_ Remote Streamable HTTP; SSE-only deprecated <<failure>>

-- Choosing an Approach
---_ Use MCP when a maintained server covers the required operations
---_ Manual schemas when no server fits or precise description control is needed
---_ Both: MCP breadth plus allowlisting and description tuning
---_ Two levers — narrow the tool set; sharpen the descriptions

